    <div class="question-block">
        <div class="question-number">4</div>
        <div class="question-text">What is HTTP 429 "Too Many Requests" and why does a system send it?</div>
        <div class="answer-section">
            <div class="answer-title">Answer: Rate Limiting to Protect Services</div>
            <div class="answer-text">
                <strong>HTTP 429</strong> is the server saying "slow down!" It happens when someone makes too many requests too fast. The server sends this to prevent abuse and protect itself from getting overwhelmed.
            </div>
            <ul class="answer-bullets">
                <li><strong>What triggers it:</strong> Example: trying to login 100 times per second, or scraping a website rapidly</li>
                <li><strong>Rate Limiting Rule:</strong> "Maximum 100 requests per minute per user" (or per IP address)</li>
                <li><strong>Counter Logic:</strong> Server counts requests. If limit exceeded → sends 429 response</li>
                <li><strong>Retry-After Header:</strong> Server includes "wait 60 seconds then try again" in response</li>
                <li><strong>Why it exists:</strong> Prevents DDoS attacks, prevents accidental crashes from buggy clients, protects API quotas</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">⏱️ Rate Limiting in Action</div>
                <pre>Request Scenario (limit: 10 req/minute):
```

Request #1 → ✓ OK (9 remaining)
Request #2 → ✓ OK (8 remaining)
Request #3 → ✓ OK (7 remaining)
…
Request #10 → ✓ OK (0 remaining)

Request #11 → ✗ HTTP 429 Too Many Requests
“Retry-After: 60”
(Come back in 60 seconds)

Request #12 → ✗ HTTP 429
Request #13 → ✗ HTTP 429
…
(wait 60 seconds)

Request #11 (after 60s) → ✓ OK (quota reset)</pre>
</div>
</div>
</div>

