        <div class="answer-section">
            <div class="answer-title">Answer: Inverted Indexes & Pre-Processing</div>
            <div class="answer-text">
                Google doesn't search the entire internet in real-time. Instead, it <strong>pre-processes all web pages offline</strong> and creates an "inverted index"—a map of which pages contain each word. Searching this index is lightning-fast.
            </div>
            <ul class="answer-bullets">
                <li><strong>Inverted Index:</strong> Map of "word → list of pages containing it" (like a book's index)</li>
                <li><strong>Offline Crawling:</strong> Google crawlers continuously download web pages 24/7</li>
                <li><strong>Indexing:</strong> Extract all words from each page, build the inverted index (happens once, stored permanently)</li>
                <li><strong>Query Time:</strong> When you search "cat", system just looks up "cat" in index, gets list of pages instantly</li>
                <li><strong>Ranking:</strong> From matching pages, rank by relevance using PageRank (pre-computed score)</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">🔍 Inverted Index for Fast Search</div>
                <pre>INVERTED INDEX (Built Offline):
```

Word “cat” →       Page1, Page5, Page99, Page150…
Word “dog” →       Page2, Page8, Page45, Page200…
Word “search” →    Page10, Page11, Page99…

QUERY TIME: User searches “cat”
✓ Lookup word “cat” in index → [Page1, Page5, Page99, Page150…]
✓ Retrieve top 10 from these pages (already ranked)
✓ Return results in ~0.3 seconds

WITHOUT INVERTED INDEX (Hypothetical):
Search “cat” → scan all 10 billion web pages
→ Find which ones have “cat”
→ Takes hours… ✗ Not feasible</pre>
</div>
</div>
</div>

