    <div class="question-block">
        <div class="question-number">15</div>
        <div class="question-text">How does a system handle cascading failures when one service crashes and causes others to fail?</div>
        <div class="answer-section">
            <div class="answer-title">Answer: Circuit Breaker & Graceful Degradation</div>
            <div class="answer-text">
                When one service crashes, systems use a <strong>circuit breaker</strong> to stop hammering it with requests. Instead, the system fails gracefully (shows cached data or simplified features) to prevent cascading failure across all services.
            </div>
            <ul class="answer-bullets">
                <li><strong>Circuit Breaker States:</strong> Closed (normal) → Open (service down, stop requests) → Half-Open (test if recovered)</li>
                <li><strong>Fail-Fast:</strong> When service A fails, don't wait for timeout—service B immediately fails and returns cached/fallback response</li>
                <li><strong>Graceful Degradation:</strong> Instead of "Error", show "offline features"; e.g., YouTube loads video without recommendations</li>
                <li><strong>Timeout Protection:</strong> Service B won't wait indefinitely for A's response; gives up after 5 seconds</li>
                <li><strong>Bulkheads:</strong> Separate thread pools for each service, so one crash doesn't exhaust all threads</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">🔄 Circuit Breaker & Cascading Failure Prevention</div>
